The Topological Geometric Origin of the Cosmic Microwave Background Continuum Spectrum
— A Non-Thermodynamic Mechanism for the Blackbody Spectrum within the TDMU Framework
Abstract

The nearly perfect blackbody continuum spectrum of the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) is a cornerstone observation of standard hot Big Bang cosmology. The standard explanation relies on repeated Compton scattering between photons and electrons in the primordial plasma to achieve thermal equilibrium. This paper, within the framework of the Topological Dynamical Membrane Universe (TDMU), proposes an alternative mechanism that does not invoke the concept of "thermal equilibrium." The main body of the CMB continuum spectrum originates from the geometric broadening of the characteristic spectral lines of the inherent defects of primitive bricks, combined with the multi-level discrete spectral lines produced by early recursive weaving and their broadening. Primitive bricks, the smallest stable weave bodies born during membrane emergence, are enormous in number and identical in structure. Their few discrete characteristic spectral lines are broadened into a continuous skeleton by the expansion of the universe and differences in propagation paths on the membrane surface. Early recursive weaving (e.g., primitive brick + primitive brick) forms new weave bodies whose number is of the same order of magnitude as primitive bricks. Their weaving scars contribute new discrete spectral lines at lower frequencies but with a total energy comparable to that of the primitive brick spectral lines. After broadening, these connect and superimpose with the primitive brick skeleton, jointly forming a continuous blackbody spectrum covering the entire frequency band. The entire process requires no introduction of "photon particles," "thermal equilibrium," or "photon-electron scattering." The origin of the continuum spectrum is attributed to the dense superposition and geometric broadening of characteristic spectral lines from weaving hierarchies. This paper demonstrates the self-consistency of this mechanism within the TDMU framework, provides testable predictions, and explicitly identifies outstanding quantitative problems to be solved.

Keywords: Topological Dynamical Membrane Universe; Cosmic Microwave Background; blackbody spectrum; primitive brick; geometric broadening; non-thermodynamic mechanism

1. Introduction
1.1 Observational Facts of the CMB and the Standard Interpretation
The spectrum of the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) is the most perfect blackbody spectrum ever measured. Results from the COBE/FIRAS satellite show that the CMB spectrum in the microwave band (peak frequency ~160 GHz, corresponding to a temperature of 2.725 K) deviates from an ideal blackbody curve by less than one part in 2000 [1]. The spectrum is extremely smooth, with no resolvable discrete emission or absorption lines.

Standard hot Big Bang cosmology explains this as follows: the early universe was a hot, dense plasma where photons were tightly coupled to charged particles (mainly electrons) through Compton scattering, achieving thermal equilibrium and forming a blackbody spectrum. As the universe cooled for about 380,000 years, protons and electrons combined to form neutral hydrogen, photons decoupled from matter, and the blackbody spectrum was "frozen," subsequently only being redshifted into the microwave band by cosmic expansion [2].

1.2 The Challenge for the TDMU Framework
The Topological Dynamical Membrane Universe (TDMU) is a cosmological framework based on a discrete combinatorial network [3]. Its core assertions are: the universe is a topological membrane emerging from a substratum; matter consists of stable loop-woven subgraphs (topological knots) on the membrane; light is a transverse wave perturbation of the orientation degree of shared links, denoted δ𝒜, and is not a particle. In this ontology, no independent "photon" entity exists, and the thermal equilibrium mechanism of photon-electron scattering cannot be directly adopted.

Therefore, TDMU must provide an alternative mechanism, fully self-consistent with its own ontology, to explain why the CMB exhibits a nearly perfect continuous blackbody spectrum.

1.3 Structure of This Paper
This paper proposes a mechanism for the origin of the CMB continuum spectrum based on the characteristic spectral lines of weaving hierarchies and geometric broadening. Section 2 clarifies the ontological status of light in TDMU. Section 3 describes the birth of primitive bricks during membrane emergence and their inherent characteristic spectral lines. Section 4 elaborates on the new multi-level spectral lines generated by early recursive weaving. Section 5 demonstrates the physical process by which geometric broadening transforms dense discrete spectral lines into a continuum. Section 6 discusses the origin of the blackbody spectral shape and outstanding quantitative problems. Section 7 presents testable predictions. Section 8 concludes.

2. The Ontological Status of Light in TDMU
In TDMU, light is not an independently existing particle, nor an entity exhibiting "wave-particle duality." Light is a dynamic transverse wave perturbation of the orientation degree of shared links, denoted δ𝒜 [4]. Its key attributes are as follows:

Light is a perturbation, not a weave body: Light contains no internal linkage zones and has no characteristic spectral lines. Its frequency is determined by the oscillation frequency of the perturbation source, not by an "internal structure" of the light.

Light propagation requires a medium: The shared link network is the propagation medium for δ𝒜. In a uniform shared link network, light propagates in straight lines without scattering or absorption.

The discreteness of light originates from port excitation: The discrete energy-frequency relationship carried by a "photon" in the Standard Model corresponds, in TDMU, to the fact that a weave body port (topological defect) can only be excited by one fundamental quantum at a time, rather than a particle called a "photon" traveling through space.

Based on this ontology, this paper does not use "photon" as an independent entity concept. All processes involving the absorption, emission, and propagation of light are described in terms of the interaction between δ𝒜 perturbations and the ports of weave bodies.

3. Primitive Bricks: The Smallest Weave Bodies at Membrane Emergence
3.1 Membrane Emergence and the Birth of Primitive Bricks
According to TDMU Postulate 2, when the loop density in the substratum exceeds a critical value, a closed membrane emerges spontaneously. Membrane emergence is a violent topological phase transition of the substratum from a non-woven state to a woven state. During this process, a vast number of loops close simultaneously, forming the first generation of stable weave bodies—the primitive bricks [5].

Primitive bricks are the smallest stable weave bodies on the membrane and are the starting point of the recursive growth tree. The weaving structure of all primitive bricks is completely identical, and their structure is fixed at the moment of the membrane's birth.

3.2 Inherent Defects of Primitive Bricks and Their Characteristic Spectral Lines
No weave body can achieve absolutely perfect closure. On the surface of a primitive brick, there exist a weaving closure scar (at the weaving closure point) and unclosed topological defects (dangling ports). These geometrically inhomogeneous regions—linkage zones—are the active sites where primitive bricks resonate with light.

Since all primitive bricks have a completely identical structure, the geometric parameters (depth, degree of stability, smoothness) of their linkage zones are also completely identical. Therefore, the inherent characteristic spectral lines contributed by all primitive bricks are the exact same few discrete spectral lines. The number of spectral lines is extremely small (possibly only 1–3 main lines) because primitive bricks are the smallest weave bodies and have a very small total number of linkage zones.

The total number of primitive bricks, N₀, is on the order of 10⁸⁰. The total radiated energy of each characteristic spectral line, E₀ ∝ N₀, represents the largest single radiation release event in cosmic history.

3.3 The Frequency of Primitive Brick Spectral Lines
The scale of a primitive brick is determined by the critical loop density at membrane emergence and is extremely small. The characteristic frequency of a weave body is inversely proportional to its scale—the smaller the scale, the smaller the resonant cavity, the greater the local stiffness, and the higher the resonant frequency. Therefore, the inherent characteristic spectral lines of primitive bricks have extremely high frequencies, far beyond the energy scale of any process in the current universe.

4. Early Recursive Weaving: Multi-Level New Spectral Lines
4.1 The Initiation of Recursive Weaving
After membrane emergence, primitive bricks do not exist in isolation. In the extremely high-energy environment of the very early universe, primitive bricks immediately combine with each other through defect-complementarity weaving (Pathway I), initiating recursive growth.

Suppose that in the first level of recursion, k₁ primitive bricks weave together into a second-generation weave body. Under ideal conditions where all primitive bricks participate in weaving, the number of second-generation weave bodies is approximately N₁ = N₀/k₁.

4.2 Weaving Scars and New Discrete Spectral Lines
After the defect-complementarity weaving between primitive bricks is complete, weaving scars are formed on the meshing interfaces—regions of the shared link network that are healed but not ideally smooth. The nature of a weaving scar differs from the inherent defects of a primitive brick:

Primitive Brick Inherent Defect	Weaving Scar
Origin	Imperfection at the closure of the primitive brick's own weave	Healed surface after defect-complementarity weaving of two weave bodies
Link State	Unclosed (dangling port) or closure scar	Saturated and closed, but with residual stress (ℐ>0)
Local Stiffness	Determined by the extremely small scale of the primitive brick	Determined by the larger scale of the combined weave body
Resonant Frequency	Extremely high	Lower than primitive bricks (larger scale, larger resonant cavity)
All second-generation weave bodies have the same structure (if the weaving mode is unique), so they contribute exactly the same set of new discrete spectral lines. The frequency of these spectral lines is lower than that of the primitive brick lines, but their total radiated energy, E₁ ∝ N₀/k₁, is of the same order of magnitude as E₀—only differing by a factor of k₁ (typically a small integer like 2, 3, or 4).

4.3 Contributions from Subsequent Recursive Levels
The second level of recursion (second-generation weave bodies + primitive bricks, or between second-generation weave bodies) produces third-generation weave bodies, numbering approximately N₂ = N₁/k₂, contributing new discrete spectral lines at even lower frequencies, with total energy E₂ reduced again slightly.

As the recursion level increases, the number of weave bodies decreases progressively, and the total energy of each level's spectral lines also decreases progressively. However, in the first few levels, the number of weave bodies is still of the same order of magnitude as the primitive bricks, or only an order of magnitude less, making the total energy of the spectral lines from each level comparable.

The discrete spectral lines generated by multi-level recursive weaving together cover a broad frequency range, from the extremely high frequencies (primitive bricks) down to lower frequencies.

5. Geometric Broadening: From Discrete Spectral Lines to a Continuous Spectrum
5.1 The Broadening Mechanism
After membrane emergence, primitive bricks and weave bodies of all levels are distributed across the membrane. The δ𝒜 perturbations released by weave bodies at different locations travel through propagation paths of varying lengths before reaching an observer. The membrane is a continuous closed surface, and the path lengths form a continuous distribution.

During propagation, the wavelength of the perturbation is continuously stretched (redshifted) due to the expansion of the universe. The longer the path, the greater the cumulative expansion stretching experienced, and the more the frequency is reduced. Discrete spectral lines with initially identical frequencies, after undergoing differential redshifts along paths of different lengths, are broadened into a continuous distribution covering a certain frequency range.

5.2 Condition for Broadening: Sufficiently Dense Spectral Lines
With only a few discrete spectral lines, even if each is broadened, the frequency gaps between them might not be fully covered. However, the early universe after membrane emergence contains a large number of discrete spectral lines of different frequencies generated by multi-level weaving:

Primitive brick spectral lines: very few in number, concentrated in an extremely high-frequency band

First-level recursion spectral lines: slightly lower in frequency

Second-level recursion spectral lines: even lower in frequency

...

The spectral lines from different levels are arranged sequentially on the frequency axis. Since there are sufficiently many recursion levels, the frequency spacing between adjacent levels is not large. Each spectral line, after geometric broadening, gains a certain width, and the broadened regions of adjacent spectral lines overlap and connect with each other, ultimately forming a seamless continuous spectrum in observation.

5.3 Energy Contribution from Each Level
The total energy of the primitive brick spectral lines, E₀, contributes the main energy in the highest frequency band of the continuous spectrum. The total energy of the first-level recursion spectral lines, E₁ ≈ E₀/k₁, contributes the main energy in a slightly lower frequency band. Subsequent recursive levels contribute to progressively lower frequency bands in turn.

Since the k-factors for the first few levels are small integers, the energy contributions from each level are of the same order of magnitude. The full-band main body of the blackbody spectrum is jointly supported by primitive bricks and early recursive weaving, rather than exhibiting a dominant "main skeleton + weak filling" relationship. The spectral lines produced by deeper recursions and later-stage cosmic processes have further reduced total energy, superimposing on the main body and serving to smooth the spectrum, but their relative intensity is so low that they are unresolvable in observation.

6. The Origin of the Blackbody Spectral Shape and Outstanding Problems
6.1 Continuity Has Been Explained
The mechanism described above explains the continuity of the CMB spectrum: a large number of discrete spectral lines of different frequencies are densely arranged and, after geometric broadening, seamlessly merge into a continuum. The entire process requires no assumption of thermal equilibrium.

6.2 The Problem of the Spectral Shape Remains to Be Solved
Why the specific functional form of the continuous spectrum is exactly the Planck blackbody radiation formula remains an open mathematical problem for TDMU.

A possible direction: if the initial frequency distribution of primitive brick spectral lines, the frequency distribution of spectral lines from various recursive levels, the statistical distribution of path lengths, and the history of cosmic expansion satisfy specific joint conditions, the broadened and superposed spectrum may mathematically approximate the Planck function form. This requires establishing a complete mathematical mapping from weaving geometry to spectral distribution.

6.3 The Geometric Counterpart of the CMB "Temperature"
In the Standard Model, the CMB temperature T = 2.725 K is a thermal equilibrium temperature. In TDMU, the physical quantity corresponding to this "temperature" is the average δ𝒜 perturbation energy released collectively by primitive bricks and early recursive weaving, redshifted to its present-day value by cosmic expansion. It is not a thermodynamic temperature but the fossil energy scale of the initial weaving event. The precise value of T should be co-determined by the weaving scale of primitive bricks, the recursive hierarchy structure, and the cosmic expansion factor.

7. Testable Predictions
Prediction 1: Minute Deviations in the CMB Spectrum at Extremely High Precision
If the CMB continuum spectrum is the result of geometric broadening and superposition rather than a product of thermal equilibrium, systematic deviations from an ideal blackbody spectrum may appear at extremely high measurement precision (better than 10⁻⁵). Such deviations would not be random noise but should reflect residual structures from the spectral lines of weaving hierarchies—for example, extremely weak undulations near certain characteristic frequencies.

Verification method: If next-generation CMB spectrum measurement experiments (like PIXIE) improve precision to the 10⁻⁶ level, they may discover systematic differences from the standard thermal blackbody spectrum at very low or very high frequency ends.

Prediction 2: Correlation between CMB Anisotropies and Membrane Topology
CMB temperature anisotropies (ΔT/T ~ 10⁻⁵) in TDMU correspond to local condition differences in different membrane surface regions during the initial and early recursive weaving. The largest-scale anisotropy patterns (low-l anomalies) should be related to the global topology of the membrane (closure mode, initial conditions of the scale factor), rather than being purely statistical fluctuations.

The Planck satellite has already observed large-scale anomalies such as a deficit in CMB power at low l [6]. TDMU predicts that such anomalies have a geometric origin, and their spatial patterns should be related to topological invariants of the membrane.

Prediction 3: Spectral Slope at the Extremely Low-Frequency End
The geometric broadening mechanism may produce a slightly different spectral slope at the extremely low-frequency end (ν → 0) compared to the Rayleigh-Jeans law (I ∝ ν²) of a thermal blackbody spectrum. The cumulative broadening effect on extremely long propagation paths may leave detectable deviations at the very low-frequency end, providing a verification window for future ultra-high-sensitivity low-frequency radio astronomy observations.

8. Conclusion
This paper has proposed, within the TDMU framework, a mechanism for the origin of the CMB continuous blackbody spectrum that does not rely on concepts such as "thermal equilibrium," "photon particles," or "photon-electron scattering":

Inherent defect characteristic spectral lines of primitive bricks: All primitive bricks (~10⁸⁰) born at membrane emergence have an identical structure. Their inherent defects contribute a very small number of discrete spectral lines with extremely high frequencies and enormous total energy, forming the main energy source in the highest frequency band of the CMB continuum spectrum.

New spectral lines from early recursive weaving: Early recursive weaving between primitive bricks produces new weave bodies whose number is of the same order of magnitude as primitive bricks. Their weaving scars contribute new discrete spectral lines at lower frequencies but with a total energy comparable to that of the primitive brick lines, covering slightly lower frequency bands.

Multi-level joint main body: The total energy of spectral lines from primitive bricks and the first few levels of recursive weaving are of the same order of magnitude, jointly constituting the full-band main body of the CMB continuum spectrum, with lines from each level arranged sequentially from high to low frequency.

Geometric broadening forms a continuous spectrum: The continuous distribution of propagation path lengths and the differential redshifts from cosmic expansion broaden the densely arranged multi-level discrete spectral lines, causing them to connect seamlessly and appear as a continuous spectrum in observation.

Deeper-level and later-stage spectral lines are submerged: The total energy of spectral lines from deeper recursion and later cosmic processes is further reduced, superimposing on the already-formed enormous continuous background. Because their relative intensity is far below 10⁻⁵, they are unresolvable in observation.

This mechanism exclusively uses native TDMU concepts, is self-consistent with the ontology of light as a δ𝒜 perturbation, and provides a purely geometric explanatory path for the CMB continuum spectrum, independent of thermodynamic equilibrium. The precise mathematical derivation of the spectral shape remains the central challenge for future work.

